---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/apps/sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/sendlane');

---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/apps" class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-300 hover:text-white">← All app reviews</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Guided ecommerce · Rank #11</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
        Sendlane is ecommerce-first email and SMS with migration support baked in — for DTC brands ready to invest in retention infrastructure but intimidated by Klaviyo blank canvases or burned by Mailchimp generic automations. Revenue-focused reporting, multi-channel journeys, and onboarding help justify entry pricing starting around $83/mo. Not for stores still proving product-market fit; right for 600+ orders/mo merchants treating lifecycle as line item, not experiment.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">
        Consider proposed evaluations for a hypothetical $140k/mo supplement brand migrating from Klaviyo, a fashion store at 720 orders/mo, and a home goods shop comparing Omnisend on price. No observed migration or merchant result is documented here. Shopify depth: {app.shopifyDepth}.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend review</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo review</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <TldrBox
      title="Verdict: Sendlane for Shopify"
      picks={[
        { label: 'Positioning', tool: 'Ecommerce-first', reason: 'Built for DTC retention — not generalist marketing with Shopify plugin.' },
        { label: 'Support', tool: 'Migration help', reason: 'Onboarding assistance reduces ESP switch terror.' },
        { label: 'Channels', tool: 'Email + SMS', reason: 'Multi-channel journeys with revenue attribution focus.' },
        { label: 'Pricing', tool: 'Premium entry', reason: 'From ~$83/mo — justify with order volume and lifecycle ROI.' },
        { label: 'Best for', tool: '600+ orders/mo', reason: 'Brands investing in retention with migration needs.' },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-white">Sendlane's niche between Omnisend and Klaviyo</h2>
      <p>
        Compare Omnisend, Klaviyo and Sendlane on the event model, migration scope, reporting and ongoing ownership your store needs. For a hypothetical supplement brand with 22k profiles, obtain matched current quotes and estimate migration labor before calculating payback. Platform savings alone do not establish improved retention, simpler maintenance or a two-month return; none of those outcomes has been measured here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sendlane does not try to be agent-first like Sequenzy or predictive-analytics-first like Klaviyo. It tries to be the platform you land on when you are done experimenting with Mailchimp and done paying Klaviyo for profiles you never segment. Custom audiences, A/B testing, deliverability tooling, and ecommerce templates cover welcome through winback without building from empty flow canvas.
      </p>
      <p>
        Transparency friction: package pricing requires sales conversation on some tiers — annoys merchants comparing Omnisend self-serve checkout. If you need price certainty before evaluation call, start Omnisend trial; if you need migration hand-holding from broken Klaviyo setup, Sendlane sales call is worth the calendar block.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card mb-12">
      <p class="badge">Shopify integration depth</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-white">Native ecommerce events and personalization</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-cyan-100">
        Sendlane connects natively to Shopify for orders, products, collections, cart events, and customer tags. Dynamic product blocks populate from cart and browse context. Custom audiences segment by purchase frequency, AOV bands, category affinity, and engagement — sufficient for most DTC retention programs without data warehouse exports.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-cyan-100">
        <strong>Standout capabilities:</strong> Multi-channel journeys (email + SMS), revenue attribution reporting, migration onboarding support, A/B testing on campaigns and automations, deliverability monitoring, cart and browse abandonment flows, post-purchase cross-sell templates, winback sequences with discount logic.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-cyan-100">
        <strong>Gaps vs Klaviyo:</strong> Predictive CLV and churn scores not native. Variant-level back-in-stock at massive SKU scale less mature. Agency recipe ecosystem smaller. MCP/agent workflows nonexistent — Sequenzy leads for AI ops teams.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-cyan-100">
        <strong>Implementation note:</strong> Migration support helps initial launch; ongoing optimization still requires internal owner. Sendlane is not Rejoiner-level managed services — software plus onboarding, not agency retainer replacement.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-white">Pricing breakdown with merchant math</h2>
      <p>
        Sendlane pricing: {app.priceDetail}. Entry from {app.pricing}; custom packages for higher volume.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hypothetical Scenario A — 720 orders/mo, 14k contacts, 38k emails, 2k SMS.</strong> Request current quotes for identical destinations, segments, carrier fees, contact rules and send volumes. Include onboarding, creative production, integrations and recurring staff labor. Compare support deliverables and reporting usability separately from sticker price; these are evaluation inputs, not verified package quotes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hypothetical Scenario B — Klaviyo migration, 22k profiles, $140k/mo supplement.</strong> Compare written proposals on the same scope and model migration labor, parallel operation and exit costs. Estimate annual savings only after checking recurring and one-time charges; no $2,160 saving or 20-hour labor reduction has been observed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hypothetical Scenario C — 380 orders/mo considering cost.</strong> Compare Omnisend and Sendlane using the same expected workload and net contribution assumptions. Order volume alone does not establish affordability or a point at which ROI turns positive; request current pricing rather than relying on an asserted merchant purchase.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-white">Four workflow tests on Shopify</h2>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">1. Klaviyo migration — cart flow rebuild with suppression</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed scenario:</strong> Supplement cart migration with duplicate-send risk. <strong>Evaluation setup:</strong> Give one sender ownership of each cart queue; disable and cancel predecessor jobs before activation. Recheck current channel and purpose permission, purchase and suppression at dispatch, and deduplicate replayed events. A fixed 14-day purchaser rule and unopened-email timer are insufficient eligibility checks.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Evaluation:</strong> Test event replay, late purchases, withdrawals and cancellation before live use. If measuring recovery, randomize eligible shoppers to comparable flow variants and report margin and uncertainty. No recovery increase or elimination of complaints has been observed.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">2. Post-purchase cross-sell — subscription plus one-time</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed scenario:</strong> A supplement order with subscription and one-time items. <strong>Evaluation setup:</strong> Separate essential order or usage information from marketing upsells. Check current subscription, purchase and purpose-specific channel permission before each message; cancel an upsell when it is no longer relevant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Evaluation:</strong> Compare subscription uptake and net contribution among randomly assigned eligible one-time buyers over a defined window. Track cancellations and refunds as well as attributed conversions; no 7.8% conversion result is documented.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">3. Browse abandonment — collection-specific email</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed scenario:</strong> Fashion shoppers browsing denim without buying. <strong>Evaluation setup:</strong> Verify identity, permitted event use and current email marketing eligibility before a collection-specific message. Recheck purchases and suppression at dispatch instead of relying solely on a fixed 30-day filter.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Evaluation:</strong> Compare browse variants under the same eligibility, assignment and observation window. Revenue per recipient is an attribution measure unless a randomized design supports a causal comparison; no same-store Omnisend or Klaviyo result has been verified here.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">4. Winback — 90-day lapsed with SMS escalation</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed scenario:</strong> Home goods customers whose buying cadence suggests possible lapse. <strong>Evaluation setup:</strong> Define a relevant window from product usage and purchase history. SMS requires its own current purpose-specific permission and suppression check; an unopened email is neither proof of inattention nor permission to escalate. Coordinate queue ownership and offer limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Evaluation:</strong> Randomize eligible customers between email-only and coordinated channels over the same period. Compare net contribution, complaints and uncertainty; a prior-year rate cannot isolate the SMS effect. No 8.4% reactivation or operational simplification has been observed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-12">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="badge">Pros</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-cyan-100">
          {app.strengths.map((s) => <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">+</span>{s}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="badge">Cons</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-cyan-100">
          {app.limits.map((l) => <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">−</span>{l}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-12">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="badge">Who should buy</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-cyan-100">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>600+ orders/mo investing in retention</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Migrating off Klaviyo or Mailchimp with anxiety</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Want email + SMS unity without enterprise pricing</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Need revenue reporting finance understands</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="badge">Who should skip</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-cyan-100">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Under 400 orders/mo — Omnisend or Sequenzy</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Need transparent self-serve pricing only</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Predictive CLV daily operating tool — Klaviyo</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-cyan-300">→</span>Agent-first MCP workflows — Sequenzy</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-white">Post-migration ownership on Sendlane</h2>
      <p>
        Sendlane onboarding gets flows live — your team owns weekly optimization afterward. Schedule monthly flow review: cart recovery rate, welcome click-to-purchase, winback margin impact. Without cadence, Sendlane becomes expensive Mailchimp with nicer templates.
      </p>
      <p>
        Document suppression rules in runbook before onboarding ends. Ask migration team to transfer segment logic explanation, not just rebuilt flows. Institutional knowledge transfer is the hidden deliverable — insist on it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-white">Proposed merchant stack evaluations with Sendlane</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed Klaviyo migration exercise:</strong> Inventory cart events, permissions and suppression rules, then agree on acceptance criteria and an internal owner. Set the launch date only after replay and cancellation checks pass; neither a two-week rebuild nor platform savings is documented.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed fashion browse exercise:</strong> Check whether quiz capture can pass the required tags and permission evidence to a collection-specific flow. Validate the actual integration and compare matched eligible cohorts before claiming an advantage over Privy plus Mailchimp.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed 380-orders/mo comparison:</strong> Include labor and migration risk in current Omnisend and Sendlane quotes. Revisit when the workload or contribution model changes, rather than assuming that doubling orders establishes a viable return.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-white">Proposed merchant stack evaluations with Sendlane</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed migration acceptance exercise:</strong> Assess cart and post-purchase flows separately, with documented event mappings, export rights and rollback ownership. Duration depends on those checks; no four-week rebuild, three-point recovery increase or month-two payback has been measured.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed fashion browse plus SMS winback exercise:</strong> Map every queue in the existing email and SMS stack before consolidation. Test duplicate events, opt-outs and purchases across both systems; no decline in complaints during a sale week is documented.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposed growth sensitivity exercise:</strong> Model the same service scope at present and forecast volumes, including message segments, fees and staff time. A forecast of 700 orders/mo within two quarters is an assumption to test, not a merchant outcome or purchasing threshold.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card mb-12">
      <p class="badge">Compare alternatives</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-white">Sendlane vs other Shopify SMS apps</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-lg font-bold">
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend review</a> — lower entry SMB multichannel</li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a> — agent-first pay-per-email alternative</li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> — data depth vs SMB speed axis</li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/rejoiner">Rejoiner review</a> — fully managed lifecycle comparison</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-8">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm font-bold">
      <a class="link" href="/apps">All Shopify SMS apps →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Cart recovery →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparison hub →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-12">
      <h2>TCPA, consent, and cost-per-message discipline</h2>
      <p>
        SMS on Shopify requires a destination-, sender- and purpose-specific review of applicable rules. Verify channel permission, legal basis, opt-in evidence and quiet-hour requirements for the actual messages. Checkout, popup and keyword wording must match live use; vendor tooling alone does not establish compliance or current capabilities.
      </p>
      <p>Model cost per delivered message before scaling broadcasts. A recovered cart that required 25% off plus three texts is often negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair urgent SMS with Sequenzy email when the message needs education, social proof, or multi-product context — never duplicate the same offer on both channels within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <p>Weekly ops: audit STOP/HELP handling, reply inbox backlog, opt-out spikes by flow, and suppression overlap with email cart recovery. Quarterly: re-read popup and checkout consent language against live automations. TCPA litigation and carrier filtering both punish stores that treat SMS like promotional email with shorter copy.</p>
    </div>



</BaseLayout>
